'use strict';
const MAP_PARTS={
 spine:['Data center: spine–leaf · Chapter 12','Servers connect to leaf switches. Each leaf connects to each spine, giving multiple paths between leaves. Spines connect leaves; this design is separate from the campus access/distribution/core model. Both describe equipment roles, not new OSI layers.','notes-12-3'],
 app:['Application · Chapter 1','This is the job closest to the program you are using. A browser requests a page; management software can request a device counter using SNMP. Application protocols give those messages a shared format.','notes-1-2'],
 presentation:['OSI 6 · Presentation','The OSI model separates representation tasks: agreeing on data formats, and concepts such as compression and encryption. In the five-layer teaching model these jobs belong with Application. This does not mean every application always encrypts or compresses.','notes-1-3'],
 session:['OSI 5 · Session','The OSI model names the job of establishing, managing and ending a conversation. The five-layer model groups this with Application. It is not an extra physical box inside a cable.','notes-1-3'],
 transport:['Transport · OSI 4','A computer can run many programs. Port numbers help deliver data to the right program. TCP can provide reliable, ordered delivery; UDP offers datagrams without TCP’s delivery guarantees. A TCP segment is a TCP header followed by its data.','notes-1-2'],
 ip:['Network / IP · OSI 3 · Chapter 4','IP gives packets source and destination IP addresses. Routers use the destination and routing table to choose the next hop. Imagine an envelope addressed across town. IPv4 has 32 bits; IPv6 has 128. ICMP carries network control and error information.','notes-4-1'],
 link:['Data Link · OSI 2 · Chapter 3','Ethernet wraps an IP packet in a frame for the local link. Source and destination MAC addresses identify interfaces on this link. A switch learns source MAC locations and forwards using destination MAC addresses. Think of delivering the envelope to the next stop.','notes-3-1'],
 physical:['Physical · OSI 1 · Chapter 2','The bits are represented as electrical signals in copper, light in fiber, or radio waves through the air. Physical does not add a giant enclosing packet: it sends a representation of the frame’s bits.','notes-2-1'],
 data:['Application data · the innermost message','This is what the application wants to communicate, including any application-protocol information. It is not “Layer 8.” The seven-layer OSI model stops at Application, Layer 7.','notes-1-4'],
 header:['Header · information before the payload','A header is a label placed before the data that a protocol carries. A TCP header includes ports; an IP header includes IP addresses; an Ethernet header includes MAC addresses. Each label serves a different delivery job.','notes-1-4'],
 payload:['Payload · the content this wrapper carries','Payload is a relative word. The whole IP packet is Ethernet’s payload. The TCP segment is the IP packet’s payload. The application data is the TCP segment’s payload. Open one box and you find another box inside.','notes-1-4'],
 fcs:['FCS · the trailer after the payload','Frame Check Sequence: a 4-byte field containing an Ethernet CRC-32 error-detection value. The receiver checks for corruption. It detects errors; it does not repair them. It is not “CRC-52.”','notes-3-3'],
 mac:['MAC address · 48 bits / 6 bytes','One hex digit represents four bits. Twelve hex digits represent 48 bits, commonly written as six pairs. For a traditional universally assigned address, the first 24 bits identify an IEEE-assigned organization prefix and the last 24 are assigned within that block. Locally assigned or randomized addresses do not necessarily identify a manufacturer.','notes-3-4'],
 cidr:['CIDR · divide an address into two parts','In 73.5.0.0/17, the slash number says the first 17 of 32 IPv4 bits are network bits. The remaining 15 are host bits. For an ordinary subnet, 2^15 = 32,768 addresses; subtract network and broadcast for 32,766 usable hosts. /31 and /32 have special rules.','notes-4-2'],
 core:['Campus core · Chapter 12','This is a NETWORK DESIGN tier: fast, reliable connections between major regions of a campus. It is not an OSI layer and not the glass core of a fiber. Think of the main highway connecting neighborhoods.','notes-12-3'],
 distribution:['Campus distribution · Chapter 12','This design tier joins access networks and is commonly a place for routing and policy. Think of roads that gather neighborhood traffic and connect it to the highway. A device here still uses the protocol stack.','notes-12-3'],
 access:['Campus access · Chapter 12','This design tier connects end devices such as computers, printers and access points. An end-user is the person using those devices. Access describes where a device serves the campus, not a numbered OSI layer.','notes-12-3'],
 building:['Buildings and structured cabling · Chapter 12','A demarcation point marks the provider/customer boundary. The MDF is the main distribution location; IDFs serve other areas or floors. Backbone cabling joins these locations. Horizontal cabling connects a local telecommunications room to work-area outlets. Patch panels organize cable terminations; they do not route packets.','notes-12-2'],
 copper:['Inside UTP copper · Chapter 2','UTP means Unshielded Twisted Pair. Typical Ethernet UTP contains four pairs of insulated copper wires inside an outer jacket. Twisting helps reduce interference and crosstalk, which is unwanted coupling between pairs. The wires carry electrical signals; the jacket protects them. Category ratings specify performance, not protocol layers.','notes-2-2'],
 fiber:['Inside optical fiber · Chapter 2',"Cladding is the glass layer directly around an optical fiber’s core. The core is the center where the light signal is guided. The cladding has different optical properties that help keep that light guided along the core. Think of a boundary that helps light stay on its path—not a metal shield or the outer plastic jacket.",'notes-2-3'],
 signals:['From bits to signals · Chapter 2','Encoding maps bits to signal patterns. Modulation changes a carrier’s amplitude, frequency or phase to represent information. Multiplexing shares a medium among streams, for example by time or frequency. Noise limits reliable communication; Shannon’s law links theoretical capacity to bandwidth and signal-to-noise ratio.','notes-2-4'],
 manager:['SNMP manager / NMS · Chapter 12','The Network Management System is software an administrator uses to monitor devices. It can send GET requests to read managed values and authorized SET requests to change supported values. These are application-layer messages carried by the same lower layers as other traffic.','notes-12-4'],
 agent:['SNMP agent · software on a device','An agent is a program on a managed switch, router or other device. It answers management requests and can send notifications such as traps. It is not a person and not a separate OSI layer.','notes-12-4'],
 mib:['MIB · names and definitions for managed values','Management Information Base: an organized set of definitions for managed objects. The agent exposes values such as interface counters according to these definitions. Think of a labeled dashboard: the MIB explains each gauge, and the agent reports its reading.','notes-12-4'],
 standards:['The people who agree on rules','IEEE develops standards including Ethernet and wireless LANs. IETF develops Internet protocol specifications. A working group is a committee of people working on a technical specification. RIRs coordinate regional Internet number allocations: allocation means assigning a block of resources. These organizations influence the network; they are not packet wrappers.','notes-12-5'],
 topology:['Topology · the shape of connections','Star: endpoints connect to a central device. Bus: devices share a common path. Ring: connections form a loop. Mesh: multiple interconnections provide alternate paths. Physical topology means actual connections; logical topology means how communication is organized. Modern switched Ethernet commonly uses a physical star; legacy shared Ethernet used a shared logical bus.','notes-3-5']
};
let mapState={tab:0,wrap:0};
const mapButton=(key,label=MAP_PARTS[key][0])=>`<button class="map-node" data-map-part="${key}">${label}</button>`;
const mapArrow=(text='connects to')=>`<div class="map-arrow">↓ <small>${text}</small></div>`;
const MAP_SECTIONS=['Whole network','Compare the models','Open the nested message','Headers & addresses','Inside the cable','Management & standards'];
function visualMapHome(){
 const tabs=MAP_SECTIONS.map((title,i)=>`<button data-map-tab="${i}" aria-pressed="${mapState.tab===i}" class="${mapState.tab===i?'active':''}">${i+1}. ${title}</button>`).join('');
 $('#app').innerHTML=`<div class="visual-map"><div class="eyebrow">CIS 320 · CONNECT THE IDEAS</div><h1>One network. Different kinds of layers.</h1><p>Start big, then zoom in. Click any labeled box to learn what it does and where it fits.</p><div class="map-legend"><span>▣ Nested boxes = contains</span><span>↓ Arrow = connects or carries</span><span>↔ Side by side = related jobs</span></div><nav class="map-tabs" aria-label="Visual map chapters">${tabs}</nav><div id="map-scene"></div><section id="map-detail" class="card map-detail" aria-live="polite"><h2>Follow the connections</h2><p>Select a labeled part of the picture for a short explanation.</p></section><div class="actions"><button id="map-prev" ${mapState.tab===0?'disabled':''}>← Previous view</button><button id="map-next" ${mapState.tab===5?'disabled':''}>Next view →</button></div><details class="card"><summary>All related lessons, organized by chapter</summary><p>The map is an overview. Open a lesson for the full study-guide detail and examples.</p>${DATA.chapters.map(c=>`<h3>Chapter ${c.id} · ${esc(c.title)}</h3><div class="jargon-chips">${DATA.notes.filter(n=>n.ch===c.id).map(n=>`<button data-lesson="${n.id}">${esc(n.title)}</button>`).join('')}</div>`).join('')}</details><details class="card"><summary>Accuracy notes & sources</summary><p>The five-layer TCP/IP teaching model maps OSI Application, Presentation and Session to one Application layer. Four-layer TCP/IP diagrams also exist. Campus tiers and cable materials are different kinds of layers.</p><p>Frame example: ordinary untagged Ethernet II carrying IPv4 and TCP; optional tags, fragmentation and other protocols change details.</p><a href="https://www.cisco.com/en/US/docs/internetworking/troubleshooting/guide/tr1904.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cisco: Ethernet frame fields</a> · <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3411" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IETF: SNMP architecture</a> · <a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/solutions/CVD/Campus/cisco-campus-lan-wlan-design-guide.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cisco: campus design</a> · <a href="https://www.thefoa.org/tech/ref/basic/total_internal_reflection.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FOA: fiber core and cladding</a> · <a href="https://www.flukenetworks.com/blog/cabling-chronicles/going-distance" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Fluke: cabling distances</a></details></div>`;
 renderMapScene();
 document.querySelectorAll('[data-map-tab]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{mapState.tab=+b.dataset.mapTab;visualMapHome()});
 $('#map-prev').onclick=()=>{mapState.tab--;visualMapHome()};$('#map-next').onclick=()=>{mapState.tab++;visualMapHome()};
 $('#map-scene').onclick=e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-map-part]');if(b)showMapPart(b.dataset.mapPart)};
}
function showMapPart(key){const [title,body,lesson]=MAP_PARTS[key];$('#map-detail').innerHTML=`<span class="tag">YOU SELECTED</span><h2>${esc(title)}</h2>${readableText(body)}<div class="actions"><button data-lesson="${lesson}">Read the connected lesson</button>${key==='cidr'?'<button data-open-decoder="73.5.0.0/17">Explore the bits live</button>':''}</div>`;$('#map-detail').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'nearest'})}
function renderMapScene(){const host=$('#map-scene');
 if(mapState.tab===0)host.innerHTML=`<h2>1 · Zoom out: a campus is a group of connected buildings</h2><p>Chapter 12 · Equipment has a place in the building AND a job in the design.</p><div class="map-columns"><section class="map-box"><h3>Design jobs · three tiers</h3>${mapButton('core','Core · campus highway')}${mapArrow('joins major regions')}${mapButton('distribution','Distribution · gathers access networks')}${mapArrow('joins local networks')}${mapButton('access','Access · connects your computer')}${mapArrow('serves')}<div class="map-chip">You → computer / printer / Wi-Fi access point</div></section><section class="map-box"><h3>Physical locations & cable path</h3>${mapButton('building','Provider → demarc → MDF')}${mapArrow('backbone cabling')}<div class="map-box">Building / floor · IDF${mapArrow('horizontal cable → outlet → patch cord')}<div class="map-chip">Computer’s network interface</div></div><p>MDF and IDF describe distribution locations. They are not automatically the core and distribution design tiers.</p>${mapButton('topology','Star · bus · ring · mesh')}</section></div><section class="map-box"><h3>Adjacent example: a data center’s spine–leaf design</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 600 170" role="img" aria-label="Two spine switches each connect to both leaf switches; each leaf connects to a server" style="width:100%;max-height:190px"><g stroke="#789284" stroke-width="3"><path d="M150 35 L150 105 M150 35 L450 105 M450 35 L150 105 M450 35 L450 105 M150 120 L150 155 M450 120 L450 155"/></g><g fill="#e0ecd9" stroke="#52715b"><rect x="90" y="10" width="120" height="40" rx="8"/><rect x="390" y="10" width="120" height="40" rx="8"/><rect x="90" y="85" width="120" height="40" rx="8"/><rect x="390" y="85" width="120" height="40" rx="8"/></g><g text-anchor="middle" fill="#163b32" font-size="17"><text x="150" y="36">Spine 1</text><text x="450" y="36">Spine 2</text><text x="150" y="111">Leaf 1</text><text x="450" y="111">Leaf 2</text><text x="150" y="170">Server</text><text x="450" y="170">Server</text></g></svg>${mapButton('spine','Why each leaf connects to each spine')}</section><div class="map-bridge">Every communicating device uses protocol jobs ↓ Explore the models next.</div><p>Separate meanings: campus core = network backbone; fiber core = glass that carries light; OSI layer = a communication job.</p>`;
 if(mapState.tab===1)host.innerHTML=`<h2>2 · Two maps of communication jobs</h2><p>Chapter 1 · Read top to bottom when sending. Read bottom to top when receiving.</p><div class="model-compare"><b>TCP/IP · guide’s 5 layers</b><b>OSI · 7 layers</b><div class="map-box application">${mapButton('app','Application')}<p>Groups the three OSI jobs at right →</p></div><div class="map-box application">${mapButton('app','7 · Application')}${mapButton('presentation','6 · Presentation')}${mapButton('session','5 · Session')}</div>${[['transport','Transport',4],['ip','Network / IP',3],['link','Data Link',2],['physical','Physical',1]].map(([k,t,n])=>`<div class="map-box ${k}">${mapButton(k,t)}</div><div class="map-box ${k}">${mapButton(k,n+' · '+(k==='ip'?'Network':t))}</div>`).join('')}</div><div class="map-bridge">These are jobs. The next view shows how the information for those jobs is wrapped.</div>`;
 if(mapState.tab===2){renderMapWrap();return}
 if(mapState.tab===3)host.innerHTML=`<h2>4 · Zoom into the labels</h2><p>Chapters 3 & 4 · Header = label before the contents. Trailer = information after the contents.</p><h3>An ordinary untagged Ethernet II frame</h3><div class="frame-strip">${mapButton('mac','Destination MAC · 6 B')}${mapButton('mac','Source MAC · 6 B')}${mapButton('header','EtherType · 2 B')}${mapButton('payload','Payload + padding · 46–1500 B')}${mapButton('fcs','FCS · 4 B')}</div><p><b>14-byte header + 46–1500-byte payload/padding + 4-byte FCS = 64–1518 bytes.</b></p><p>EtherType identifies the carried protocol, such as IPv4. The 7-byte preamble and 1-byte start delimiter precede the frame and are excluded from that 64–1518-byte count.</p><div class="map-columns"><section class="map-box"><h3>MAC · local-link address</h3>${mapButton('mac','00:1A:2B | 3C:4D:5E')}<p>24-bit organization prefix | 24-bit assigned extension, for the traditional universally assigned form.</p><div class="map-chip">One hex digit A → four bits 1010</div><p>Two hex digits = 8 bits = one byte.</p></section><section class="map-box"><h3>IP · address used across networks</h3>${mapButton('cidr','73.5.0.0 /17')}<div class="map-bits"><span>01001001.00000101.0</span><strong>0000000.00000000</strong></div><p>First 17 bits: network (blue). Last 15: host (green). Each dotted IPv4 part is an octet: 8 bits.</p>${mapButton('transport','Ports · the program at the destination')}</section></div><div class="map-bridge">Ports → program · IP → across networks · MAC → next local delivery</div>`;
 if(mapState.tab===4)host.innerHTML=`<h2>5 · Zoom into what physically carries the bits</h2><p>Chapter 2 · These are actual material layers. A packet is a pattern of information, not a tiny physical box moving through the wire.</p><div class="map-columns"><section class="map-box"><h3>Copper cable cross-section</h3><div class="cable-shell"><span>Outer jacket</span><div class="copper-pairs" aria-label="Four pairs, each with two insulated copper conductors">${['Blue','Orange','Green','Brown'].map((c,i)=>`<div class="wire-pair pair-${i}"><i>●</i><i>●</i><small>${c} pair</small></div>`).join('')}</div></div>${mapButton('copper','Zoom: jacket → insulation → copper')}<p>Along the cable, the two wires in each pair twist around one another. The cross-section shows their cut ends.</p></section><section class="map-box"><h3>Optical fiber cross-section</h3><div class="fiber-jacket">Jacket / protection<div class="fiber-coating">Coating<div class="fiber-cladding">Cladding · guides light at the boundary<div class="fiber-core">Core · light</div></div></div></div>${mapButton('fiber','Zoom: protection → cladding → core')}<p>Diagram is not to scale. Cable construction varies; cables may contain multiple protected fibers.</p></section></div>${mapButton('physical','Copper: electrical ↔ Fiber: light ↔ Wireless: radio')}${mapButton('signals','Encoding → modulation → shared medium')}<p>Single-mode and multimode describe light propagation in fiber, not OSI layers. For common balanced-copper structured cabling, the channel is up to 100 m, typically 90 m permanent link plus 10 m of cords; supported distance still depends on the standard and installation.</p>`;
 if(mapState.tab===5)host.innerHTML=`<h2>6 · Who watches the network, and who writes the rules?</h2><p>Chapter 12 · These sit beside the network map because they manage or define its operation.</p><div class="map-columns"><section class="map-box"><h3>Management conversation</h3>${mapButton('manager','NMS · administrator’s management software')}${mapArrow('GET reads · SET requests a supported change')}<div class="map-box">Managed switch / router${mapButton('agent','Agent · software answering requests')}${mapButton('mib','MIB · definitions of managed objects')}</div><div class="map-arrow">↑ <small>Responses & notifications return to the manager</small></div><p>Typical path: SNMP message → UDP → IP → local-link frame → signals. Management uses the network’s layers too.</p></section><section class="map-box"><h3>Standards and address coordination</h3>${mapButton('standards','People in working groups → shared specifications')}<div class="map-chip">IEEE → Ethernet / wireless LAN rules</div><div class="map-chip">IETF → Internet protocol specifications</div><div class="map-chip">RIRs → regional IP address allocations</div><p>Rules let different products communicate. An allocation is an assigned block of addresses; a public IP is used for globally unique Internet addressing.</p></section></div><div class="map-bridge">Connect it all: people define rules → software follows them → devices wrap messages → signals carry them through cables or air.</div>`;
}
function renderMapWrap(){const steps=[['Message','Start with the application’s message. This is the innermost doll.'],['TCP segment','Transport adds a TCP header. Its ports help identify the sending and receiving programs.'],['IP packet','Network adds an IP header around the entire TCP segment.'],['Ethernet frame','Data Link adds an Ethernet header before the IP packet and an FCS trailer after it.'],['Signals','Physical represents the frame’s bits as signals. It does not add another enclosing data box.'],['At the receiver','The receiver interprets signals, checks and removes the link wrapper, processes the IP header, and delivers transport data to the application.']];let inner=`<div class="map-box application">${mapButton('data','Application data · your message')}</div>`;if(mapState.wrap>=1)inner=`<div class="map-box transport">${mapButton('transport','TCP header · ports')}<small>TCP payload contains ↓</small>${inner}</div>`;if(mapState.wrap>=2)inner=`<div class="map-box ip">${mapButton('ip','IP header · IP addresses')}<small>IP payload contains ↓</small>${inner}</div>`;if(mapState.wrap>=3)inner=`<div class="map-box link">${mapButton('link','Ethernet header · MAC addresses')}<small>Ethernet payload contains ↓</small>${inner}${mapButton('fcs','FCS trailer · error check')}</div>`;
 $('#map-scene').innerHTML=`<h2>3 · The Russian doll: information inside information</h2><p>Chapters 1, 3 & 4 · Example: application data carried by TCP, IPv4 and Ethernet.</p><div class="map-wrap-controls">${steps.map(([t],i)=>`<button data-wrap="${i}" aria-pressed="${mapState.wrap===i}">${i+1}. ${t}</button>`).join('')}</div><div class="map-bridge" aria-live="polite"><b>${steps[mapState.wrap][0]}</b><p>${steps[mapState.wrap][1]}</p></div><div class="doll-wrap">${inner}</div>${mapState.wrap>=4?`<div class="map-arrow">${mapState.wrap===5?'↑ receive and unwrap':'↓ encode and send'}</div><div class="map-signals">010101… → electrical / optical / radio signals</div>`:''}<p>Boxes show containment, not exact byte sizes. UDP uses a UDP header instead of a TCP header. OSI Presentation and Session do not each require a separate universal wrapper.</p>${hopJourneyHTML()}<button data-lesson="notes-hop-delivery">Read the hop-by-hop lesson</button><details><summary>What happens at a router along the way?</summary><p>A router removes the incoming link wrapper, processes the IP packet and sends it in a new outgoing link frame. The local MAC delivery changes at routed hops; the IP destination normally still identifies the final host (address translation is a separate case).</p></details>`;
 document.querySelectorAll('[data-wrap]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{mapState.wrap=+b.dataset.wrap;renderMapWrap()});
}
